      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the number of women who attended the party. Let's break down the information given:\n\n1. Each man danced with exactly three women.\n2. Each woman danced with exactly two men.\n3. There were twelve men at the party.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the total number of dances that occurred at the party. Since each man danced with three women, and there are twelve men, the total number of dances can be calculated as follows:\n\n\\[ \\text{Total dances} = 12 \\text{ men} \\times 3 \\text{ women per man} = 36 \\text{ dances} \\]\n\nNext, let's determine the total number of \"man-woman dance pairs.\" Each dance involves one man and one woman, so the total number of man-woman dance pairs is equal to the total number of dances:\n\n\\[ \\text{Total man-woman dance pairs} = 36 \\]\n\nNow, we need to find out how many women attended the party. Each woman danced with exactly two men. Let \\( w \\) represent the number of women who attended the party. Since each woman danced with two men, the total number of man-woman dance pairs can also be expressed as:\n\n\\[ \\text{Total man-woman dance pairs} = 2w \\]\n\nWe know from our previous calculation that the total number of man-woman dance pairs is 36. Therefore, we can set up the following equation:\n\n\\[ 2w = 36 \\]\n\nTo find \\( w \\), we solve for \\( w \\):\n\n\\[ w = \\frac{36}{2} = 18 \\]\n\nSo, the number of women who attended the party is \\(\\boxed{18}\\).",
